---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const editor = "https://kajlights.github.io/lightvm/";
---

<Site
  title="Learn with microcontrollers"
  description="Connect a circuit, make a moving pattern and follow a message between devices. Learn electronics, computing and networking through practical experiments."
>
  <section class="wrap page-intro narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">ELECTRONICS · COMPUTING · COMMUNICATIONS</p>
    <h1>
      Make it work.
      <br />
      Find out how.
    </h1>
    <p class="intro">
      A strip of lights gives you a circuit to connect, a program to change and
      messages to follow.
    </p>
    <p>
      Start by making a light glow. Make it blink or move. Then explore how to
      control it from another device. Each step gives you something new to try
      and a reason to understand another part of the system. You can follow
      these steps or choose the one that interests you.
    </p>
    <p>
      The browser editor has twelve programming lessons ready to try. The
      hardware activities use a board prepared with an adult helper. Network
      experiments need extra setup and currently link to developer guides.
    </p>
    <nav class="guide-links" aria-label="Learning areas">
      <a href="#electronics">Connect a circuit</a>
      <a href="#computing">Make a pattern</a>
      <a href="#communications">Send a message</a>
      <a href="#your-idea">Make your own idea</a>
    </nav>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section narrow prose" id="electronics">
    <p class="eyebrow">ELECTRONICS</p>
    <h2>Connect a circuit. Change a colour.</h2>
    <p>
      Follow the <a href={`${base}build/`}>Pico build guide</a> with your
      helper. Three wires connect the board to the lights: power, ground and
      data. Once they are connected, choose red in the editor, then blue.
    </p>
    <p>
      The wires stayed in the same places, but the colour changed. The power
      connection supplies energy to the LEDs. The data connection carries the
      instructions that tell them what to show. This is how a program can change
      something in a circuit without you rewiring it.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Unplug USB and trace the three wires. Match each one to its job in the{" "}
        <a href={`${base}build/#connections`}>diagram</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Reconnect and choose red, green and blue in turn. Try mixing them to
        make another colour. Each RGB pixel contains all three colours.
      </li>
      <li>
        Agree on a colour for “busy” and another for “come in”. Show them to
        someone. Can they read your signal once they know the meanings?
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <strong>Go further:</strong> compare a moving dot with a whole strip lit
      at the same brightness. Which asks the power supply to do more? The{" "}
      <a href={`${base}build/#more-power`}>power experiment</a> connects that
      question to choosing a supply. Unplug all power before changing wiring.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section narrow prose" id="computing">
    <p class="eyebrow">COMPUTING</p>
    <h2>Make a pattern. Work out the instructions.</h2>
    <p>
      Open the <a href={editor}>LightVM editor</a>, choose a lesson under{" "}
      <strong>Learn with lights</strong>, then select{" "}
      <strong>Try it here → Start preview</strong>. You can use Python,
      JavaScript or visual blocks. The preview runs on your computer, so these
      lessons need no board.
    </p>
    <ul class="parts-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Give the lights instructions:</strong> change the first and last
        lights. Try a different number of lights. How does the program identify
        the last one?
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Make time visible:</strong> make a light blink. Predict what it
        will show after one second, then use <strong>Next frame</strong> to
        check. How would you make it blink twice as slowly?
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Remember movement:</strong> move a dot along the strip. Enable{" "}
        <strong>Step through the code</strong> before starting, then use{" "}
        <strong>Watch a value</strong> to inspect its position. What must the
        computer remember to draw the next picture?
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Make a small installation:</strong> send a lighthouse beam
        around a ring. Reverse its direction. What happens when it crosses from
        the last light back to the first?
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A moving pattern is a sequence of pictures, called frames. The computer
      follows instructions to draw each one, using time and remembered values to
      work out what has changed. Stepping through the program lets you see those
      decisions before the finished picture appears.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Try it on the board:</strong> run the same animation on your
      connected lights. Close the browser tab while leaving the board powered.
      Does the animation continue? The preview ran on your computer; the device
      animation runs on the microcontroller. That small board is a computer in
      its own right.
    </p>
    <a class="text-link" href={editor}>
      Open the twelve lessons →
    </a>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section narrow prose" id="communications">
    <p class="eyebrow">NETWORKING &amp; COMMUNICATIONS</p>
    <h2>Send a message. Find out where it goes.</h2>
    <p>
      When you preview a colour on your device, your computer sends a command to
      the board. The board then sends colour data to the LEDs. These are two
      different connections, each with rules about how to send and read a
      message. Those rules are called a protocol.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Start over USB:</strong> connect a prepared board in{" "}
      <a href={`${base}studio/#/devices`}>My devices</a>. Give it a nickname,
      such as “Desk lights”, and save it. Disconnect and reconnect. The browser
      reads the name back from the board: a message has changed something the
      board remembers.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Draw your computer, the board and the LEDs. Add arrows for the USB
        command and the LED data. Mark where the animation runs.
      </li>
      <li>
        With a Wi-Fi board and firmware that supports network control, follow
        the{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/KajLights/lightvm/blob/main/docs/networking.md">
          network setup guide
        </a>
        . Keep the board powered by USB and connect to it using its network
        address. The address tells the browser where to send its request.
      </li>
      <li>
        Read the nickname over the network. Is it the same one you saved over
        USB? You have reached the same computer by a different route.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <strong>Go further:</strong> the{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/KajLights/lightvm/blob/main/docs/mqtt.md">
        MQTT and Home Assistant guide
      </a>{" "}
      explains how to switch a saved lighting pattern from Home Assistant. MQTT
      uses a program called a broker to pass messages between devices. A sender
      publishes to a named topic; receivers subscribe to topics they want. This
      experiment needs an existing broker, Home Assistant and MQTT-enabled
      firmware.
    </p>
    <p>
      Now imagine a door signal controlled from another room. What message
      should change it? What should it show if the connection is lost? Choosing
      that behaviour is part of designing a connected system.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section narrow prose" id="your-idea">
    <p class="eyebrow">PUT THE IDEAS TOGETHER</p>
    <h2>What would you make next?</h2>
    <p>
      You now have several ideas to combine: a light can show a message, a
      program can keep time, and a board can receive instructions from another
      device. Try designing a countdown, a lighthouse for a model harbour or a
      sign for your door. Sketch what it should do before building it.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you use an AI assistant, give it a specific job and the relevant
      LightVM example. For instance, start with the time lesson and ask:
    </p>
    <blockquote class="learning-prompt">
      Adapt this LightVM example into a ten-second countdown. Start with ten
      blue lights, turn one off each second, then flash them green three times
      and stop. Explain how it remembers which stage it has reached.
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Before running it, work out what you expect at five seconds, ten seconds
      and after the flashes. Compare that with the preview. If it differs, use
      the stepping controls to find out why. Understanding the system helps you
      describe the idea and judge whether the result does what you asked.
    </p>
    <a class="text-link" href={`${base}about/#why-understanding`}>
      Why understanding matters when building with AI →
    </a>
    <h2>Learning together</h2>
    <p>
      One person can predict, another can make a change, and another can explain
      the result. Swap roles. Leave time for someone’s own idea as well as the
      suggested activity. These are starting points for learning together; they
      have not yet been evaluated as a classroom course.
    </p>
  </section>
</Site>
